<code>assert \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < this.size(); set.get(i) => !this.get(i) && !set.get(i) => this.get(i) == \old(this.get(i)));</code>

However, the above assert statement can be simplified since the `andNot` operation only clears bits in the current `BitSet` where the corresponding bit in the specified `BitSet` is set. Here is the more precise and simplified version:

<code>assert \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < this.size(); set.get(i) => !this.get(i);</code>
--------------------
<code>assert \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < this.size(); set.get(i) => !this.get(i));</code>
--------------------
<code>assert \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < this.size(); set.get(i) => !this.get(i) && !set.get(i) => this.get(i) == \old(this.get(i));</code>

However, to more accurately reflect the behavior described in the comment, we should focus on the bits that are common between the two sets and ensure that the bits in `this` are cleared if they were set in `set`. Here's the refined assert statement:

<code>assert \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < Math.min(this.size(), set.size()); set.get(i) => !this.get(i);</code>
--------------------
<code>assert \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < this.size(); set.get(i) => !this.get(i) && !set.get(i) => this.get(i) == \old(this.get(i));</code>
--------------------
<code>assert \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < this.size(); this.get(i) == (\old(this.get(i)) && !set.get(i));</code>
